WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.133
Context managers.

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:07.033
They guarantee that setup and teardown happen, even when something goes wrong in the middle.

3
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:10.800
The with statement guarantees setup and teardown.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.833
Files close, locks release, and connections go back to the pool.

5
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:16.933
Even if an exception is raised.

6
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:19.300
Here's the sequence.

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.567
Enter runs first, doing the setup.

8
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:23.200
Then your block runs.

9
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.000
Exit always runs afterward, even on the path where an exception was raised.

10
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.633
Only then does the exception travel on.

11
00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:34.000
You could write try and finally by hand.

12
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.467
But then cleanup is yours to remember.

13
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:39.215
With a context manager, it's built in.

14
00:00:39.215 --> 00:00:43.467
And the easy way to write one uses a decorator and a single yield.

15
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:45.967
Here's a stage timer.

16
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:48.522
Everything before the yield is the enter.

17
00:00:48.522 --> 00:00:49.567
We start the clock.

18
00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:52.763
Everything after, inside finally, is the exit.

19
00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:54.400
We print the elapsed time.

20
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.800
And the with block is timed even if embed raises.

21
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.333
Here's why teardown guarantees are production guarantees.

22
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:09.133
Langfuse tracing wraps each RAG stage in a span, so timing is recorded even when the reranker throws.

23
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:12.433
Database connections come from a pool the same way.

24
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:18.733
Once, someone skipped with, an exception leaked a connection, the pool drained, and the whole service froze.

25
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:22.667
The mistake is acquiring a resource manually.

26
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:25.133
The fix is simple: always use with.

27
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:27.065
Let's recap.

28
00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:29.200
With guarantees setup and teardown.

29
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.867
Exit always runs.

30
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:33.933
Write your own with the contextmanager decorator.

31
00:01:34.233 --> 00:01:36.100
Next up: exception handling.
